## 2026-07-20: 009 — Framing addition: cross-surface portability use case

Creative lead added a motivating use case to the standardization argument:
applications that span surfaces with no shared UI standard (e.g. Claude
Code / Codex as native desktop, web, IDE extension, and CLI) — under NNUIC,
a window dragged from one surface to another is a cell moving between
vessels.

Reduced to mechanism before admission (per Invariant 7 discipline): a cell's
complete description is genome Γ + material state {S, n, ξ, D} + containment;
no geometry is stored because none is ever authored (Invariant 1), so the
cell is host-portable by construction. Cross-surface "drag" = serialize →
inject into a new vessel → re-express → re-settle under local pressure (the
mitosis re-expression mechanism doing cross-surface work), with R2
determinism guaranteeing identical settling on every host.

Recorded in `README.md` as motivating use case only. Boundary held: actual
OS/host integration remains explicitly deferred (stage-5) until the
agent-facing API (workstream A) exists.

